  "body": "## What changed\n\nSince #54 / PR #210, the rotary wrap refused by name a non-turned feature (a D-flat or keyway) that crossed its seam, so the user had to rotate the work. Now, when the default seam (the basis' ±π branch) is refused, the wrap looks for a seam angle itself:\n\n- **Candidates** are the midpoints of the gaps between vertex azimuth columns. Each is judged by the unchanged #54/#228 seam law. A facet straddles a candidate when the candidate lies inside the shortest arc holding its off-axis vertices; one sweep hands every candidate its straddlers.\n- **The pick** is the turned candidate farthest from any refused one, which on a D-flat is opposite the flat. Ties go to the candidate nearest the default seam.\n- **The wrap** turns the azimuth basis about the axis by that angle and lays the development out again. The turned sheet is judged again before anything is cut. The world exit maps back through the same turned basis, so only the split moves and every point is cut at its true azimuth.\n- **Unchanged outputs:** when the default seam is already turned it is kept, so existing wraps are byte-identical. No golden changed.\n- **The refusal** happens only when no angle works (a feature all the way round), and says so by name. The search also refuses by name after handing out 20M straddler vertices, so a very dense mesh can't hang it.\n\nCode: new `rotary_seam_angle.rs` (the search) and `rotary_sheet.rs` (the lap loop moved out of `rotary_offset.rs`, which shrinks from 401 to about 317 lines; plus `Basis::turned` and `polar`). `rotary_seam.rs` is split into `seam_columns` (the verdict) and `seam_refusal` (the error). Decisions are recorded in `docs/decisions/226-wrap-seam-angle.md`.\n\n## Readings\n\n- All 36 off-centre flat placements wrap with 0 cut tips inside the true cross-section. Channel tips stand at the flat's true azimuth.\n- A facet spread over more than half a turn (for example a non-fan end cap) makes the search pessimistic. Such a part may still be refused, but never unsafely, because the full development is judged again.\n- Only Offset rings is checked for tips on world output. The Adaptive case checks that the developed channel exists; the Adaptive heavy lane (`rotary_adaptive` ignored tests) was not run.\n\n## Pinning tests (`crates/motion/tests/rotary_dflat.rs`)\n\n- `an_off_centre_flat_across_the_seam_never_puts_a_tip_in_the_part`: all 36 placements now wrap.\n- `a_d_flat_bar_wraps_in_every_placement`: no refusal escape; tips at the flat's azimuth.\n- `a_flat_channel_across_the_default_seam_develops_under_adaptive_and_rings` replaces `a_flat_channel_across_the_seam_is_refused_by_name_before_the_wrap`.\n- `a_feature_all_the_way_round_is_still_refused_by_name` (new).\n- Unit tests: `the_shortest_arc_runs_across_the_zero_azimuth`, `the_candidates_include_the_gap_across_zero`.\n\nGate: `scripts/gate.sh quick` (macOS): motion suites green apart from the known `swarf_pose_continuity` (pre-existing). Also run: app `rotary_verify`, `center_cut_multiaxis`, `project_roundtrip`, `operations` (green). clippy `-p camcraft-motion --all-targets -D warnings` clean.\n\nChangelog: The rotary wrap turns its seam clear of a D-flat or keyway, so a flatted bar roughs in any placement; only a feature all the way round is refused (#226)\n\nFixes #226\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
